          "statement": "Find the equation of the right circular cone whose vertex is at the point $V(1, 2, 3)$, whose axis has direction ratios $(2, -1, 2)$, and whose semi-vertical angle is $\\alpha = 45^\\circ$.",
          "solution": "**Step 1: Compute the direction cosines of the axis**  \nThe direction ratios are $(2, -1, 2)$. The magnitude is:\n$$\\sqrt{2^2 + (-1)^2 + 2^2} = \\sqrt{4 + 1 + 4} = \\sqrt{9} = 3$$\n\nDirection cosines: $(l, m, n) = \\left(\\frac{2}{3}, -\\frac{1}{3}, \\frac{2}{3}\\right)$.\n\n---\n\n**Step 2: Set up the angle condition**  \nLet $P(x, y, z)$ be any point on the cone. The vector from vertex $V(1, 2, 3)$ to $P$ is:\n$$\\vec{VP} = (x - 1)\\hat{i} + (y - 2)\\hat{j} + (z - 3)\\hat{k}$$\n\nThe cosine of the angle between $\\vec{VP}$ and the axis is $\\cos 45^\\circ = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}$:\n$$\\cos 45^\\circ = \\frac{\\frac{2}{3}(x - 1) - \\frac{1}{3}(y - 2) + \\frac{2}{3}(z - 3)}{\\sqrt{(x - 1)^2 + (y - 2)^2 + (z - 3)^2}}$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by 3:\n$$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\cdot 3 \\sqrt{(x - 1)^2 + (y - 2)^2 + (z - 3)^2} = [2(x - 1) - (y - 2) + 2(z - 3)]$$\n\n---\n\n**Step 3: Square both sides**  \n$$\\frac{9}{2} \\left[ (x - 1)^2 + (y - 2)^2 + (z - 3)^2 \\right] = [2x - y + 2z - (2 - 2 + 6)]^2$$\n$$\\frac{9}{2} \\left[ (x - 1)^2 + (y - 2)^2 + (z - 3)^2 \\right] = (2x - y + 2z - 6)^2$$\n\nMultiply by 2:\n$$9 \\left[ (x - 1)^2 + (y - 2)^2 + (z - 3)^2 \\right] = 2(2x - y + 2z - 6)^2$$\n\nThis is the exact Cartesian equation of the right circular cone."
