        {
          "id": "sec-geom3d-6-3",
          "title": "6.3 Tangent Planes, Polar Planes, and Diametral Systems",
          "content": "### 1. Tangent Plane to a Central Conicoid\n\nLet the central conicoid be:\n\n$$A x^2 + B y^2 + C z^2 = 1$$\n\nLet $P(x_1, y_1, z_1)$ be a point on the surface. The gradient vector at $P$ is:\n\n$$\\nabla F = (2A x_1, 2B y_1, 2C z_1)$$\n\nThe tangent plane at $P$ is perpendicular to $\\nabla F$:\n\n$$A x_1 (x - x_1) + B y_1 (y - y_1) + C z_1 (z - z_1) = 0$$\n$$A x x_1 + B y y_1 + C z z_1 = A x_1^2 + B y_1^2 + C z_1^2$$\n\nSince $(x_1, y_1, z_1)$ lies on the conicoid, the right-hand side equals 1:\n\n$$\\mathbf{A x x_1 + B y y_1 + C z z_1 = 1}$$\n\n---\n\n### 2. Condition of Tangency of a Plane\n\nLet a plane be given by:\n\n$$l x + m y + n z = p$$\n\nIf this plane is tangent to $A x^2 + B y^2 + C z^2 = 1$ at $(x_1, y_1, z_1)$, its coefficients must be proportional to the tangent plane equation:\n\n$$\\frac{A x_1}{l} = \\frac{B y_1}{m} = \\frac{C z_1}{n} = \\frac{1}{p}$$\n\nSolving for the contact point:\n\n$$x_1 = \\frac{l}{A p}, \\quad y_1 = \\frac{m}{B p}, \\quad z_1 = \\frac{n}{C p}$$\n\nSubstituting these coordinates into the conicoid equation $A x_1^2 + B y_1^2 + C z_1^2 = 1$:\n\n$$A \\left(\\frac{l}{A p}\\right)^2 + B \\left(\\frac{m}{B p}\\right)^2 + C \\left(\\frac{n}{C p}\\right)^2 = 1$$\n$$\\frac{l^2}{A p^2} + \\frac{m^2}{B p^2} + \\frac{n^2}{C p^2} = 1$$\n\n$$\\mathbf{\\frac{l^2}{A} + \\frac{m^2}{B} + \\frac{n^2}{C} = p^2}$$\n\nFor an ellipsoid $\\frac{x^2}{a^2} + \\frac{y^2}{b^2} + \\frac{z^2}{c^2} = 1$ ($A = 1/a^2, B = 1/b^2, C = 1/c^2$), the tangency condition is:\n\n$$\\mathbf{a^2 l^2 + b^2 m^2 + c^2 n^2 = p^2}$$\n\n---\n\n### 3. The Director Sphere\n\n**Theorem:**  \nThe locus of the point of intersection of three mutually perpendicular tangent planes to a central conicoid $A x^2 + B y^2 + C z^2 = 1$ is a concentric sphere called the **director sphere**:\n\n$$\\mathbf{x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = \\frac{1}{A} + \\frac{1}{B} + \\frac{1}{C}}$$\n\nFor the canonical ellipsoid $\\frac{x^2}{a^2} + \\frac{y^2}{b^2} + \\frac{z^2}{c^2} = 1$:\n\n$$\\mathbf{x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = a^2 + b^2 + c^2}$$"
        }
