          "title": "Tangent Planes Parallel to a Given Plane",
          "statement": "Find the equations of the two tangent planes to the ellipsoid:\n$$2x^2 + 6y^2 + 3z^2 = 6$$\nthat are parallel to the plane:\n$$2x - 3y + 3z = 0$$\nAlso find the perpendicular distance between these two parallel tangent planes.",
          "solution": "**Step 1: Write ellipsoid in canonical form**  \nDivide $2x^2 + 6y^2 + 3z^2 = 6$ by 6:\n$$\\frac{x^2}{3} + \\frac{y^2}{1} + \\frac{z^2}{2} = 1$$\nHere $a^2 = 3, b^2 = 1, c^2 = 2$.\n\n---\n\n**Step 2: Set up parallel planes**  \nAny plane parallel to $2x - 3y + 3z = 0$ has the form:\n$$2x - 3y + 3z = p$$\nHere direction numbers are $l = 2, m = -3, n = 3$.\n\n---\n\n**Step 3: Apply the condition of tangency**  \nFor the ellipsoid $\\frac{x^2}{a^2} + \\frac{y^2}{b^2} + \\frac{z^2}{c^2} = 1$ and plane $l x + m y + n z = p$:\n$$p^2 = a^2 l^2 + b^2 m^2 + c^2 n^2$$\n\nSubstitute values:\n$$p^2 = 3(2^2) + 1(-3)^2 + 2(3^2)$$\n$$p^2 = 3(4) + 1(9) + 2(9) = 12 + 9 + 18 = 39$$\n\nTherefore:\n$$p = \\pm \\sqrt{39}$$\n\nThe two tangent planes are:\n$$2x - 3y + 3z = \\sqrt{39} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 2x - 3y + 3z = -\\sqrt{39}$$\n\n---\n\n**Step 4: Distance between the parallel planes**  \nThe distance between $2x - 3y + 3z = \\sqrt{39}$ and $2x - 3y + 3z = -\\sqrt{39}$ is:\n$$D = \\frac{|p_1 - p_2|}{\\sqrt{l^2 + m^2 + n^2}} = \\frac{2\\sqrt{39}}{\\sqrt{2^2 + (-3)^2 + 3^2}} = \\frac{2\\sqrt{39}}{\\sqrt{4 + 9 + 9}} = \\frac{2\\sqrt{39}}{\\sqrt{22}}$$\n$$D = \\sqrt{\\frac{78}{11}} \\approx 2.66 \\text{ units}$$"
